import json,collections
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];a=json.loads((R/'output/atlas.json').read_text());s=a['stats'];nodes=a['nodes']
lines=['# Vedic Atlas: analysis and coverage','',f"Built on 2 November 2026 from the 30 PDFs supplied in `inputs/`. The corpus contains **7,984 physical PDF pages**. The graph contains **{s['nodes']:,} nodes and {s['edges']:,} directed relationships**.",'','## Open and navigate','','Open [the interactive atlas](../output/vedic-atlas.html). The library runs in the browser and is also available as a complete local copy. Keep the output files together and preserve the relative `inputs` folder for original-PDF links.','','- Library: browse text families, works, books, hymns, stories, chapters, and source editions.','- Connections: explore recurring figures, ideas, and narratives across the supplied sources.','- Passages: search every extracted or recognized source page. Spellings and diacritics vary.','- Coverage: inspect partial editions, overlaps, OCR, commentary, and known gaps.','- Click a node to focus its neighborhood. The map pages six children at a time; the library and detail list expose the rest. Drag to pan, scroll to zoom, or use Fit.','- Open source PDF links use one-based physical PDF pages, not printed page or verse numbering.','- Download the current view as SVG, or use the [complete GraphML graph](../output/atlas.graphml) and [JSON data](../output/atlas.json).','','## What completeness means','','Every input file and every physical PDF page has a graph record. The supplied Muktika catalog has all 108 entries mapped. All reliably extracted structural units are indexed, including the 1,028 Rigveda hymns and all observed main chapter starts in the two scanned Purana volumes.','','This is **complete source coverage with a structured index and selected semantic analysis**. It is not an exhaustive scholarly annotation of every verse, embedded story, theological claim, or cross-text parallel. Some supplied files are summaries, partial editions, or drafts. Missing material has not been invented or imported. A heading labeled story is a navigation classification, not proof that every narrative unit has been separately interpreted.','','## Main findings','','The collection is best understood as several connected forms of literature rather than one uniform sequence of stories. The Veda files contain hymn, chant, and ritual-formula collections. The Upanishad compilation adds philosophical dialogues, contemplative instructions, renunciant teaching, and texts centered on named deities. The Purana files organize narratives alongside cosmology, genealogy, ritual, devotion, and pilgrimage. The practical guides form a separate modern layer.','','Names such as Agni, Indra, Rudra, Vishnu, Yama, and Narada provide useful navigation across families, but their roles and meanings depend on the passage. Shared names and words alone do not establish one doctrine or direct literary borrowing. Brahma and Brahman are kept separate. The name Rama is flagged as potentially ambiguous.','','The Rig/Sama comparison identifies **472 English phrase-overlap links** with source evidence from both supplied PDFs. These are translation-level observations, not verified Sanskrit verse equivalences or proof of historical dependence.','','The Nachiketa reading path compares the Katha dialogue with the Varaha retelling. Other curated recurring cycles include Daksha and Sati, ocean churning, Matsya and the deluge, Dhruva, Prahlada and Narasimha, Vamana and Bali, Ganga, Krishna, and Rama. These remain parallel-account or theme links unless the source explicitly describes a different relationship.','','## Indexed content','','| Collection | Supplied coverage and indexed units |','|---|---|','| Vedas | 6 PDFs; 1,028 Rigveda hymns; 433 Whitney Atharva hymns in Books I–VII; 32 selected later-book Bloomfield hymns; 460 supplied Sama song groups; 650 observed Keith numbered passages; 40 Tulsi Ram Yajur chapters. |','| Upanishads | 1 compiled PDF; all 108 catalog texts; 31 selected story/dialogue entries; the ten Nirmalananda Giri commentaries separated; Gaudapada Karika separately identified. |','| Puranas | 20 PDFs representing 18 named works; source headings, 35 Skanda chapters, all 128 main Matsya chapters plus appendix chapters 271–273, and 31 Brahmanda continuation chapters. |','| Practical guides | 3 PDFs; 25 Ayurveda ingredient topics, 44 Vastu page topics, and all 51 supplied yoga PDF pages. |','','Counts refer to observed supplied units and may use edition-specific numbering; they are not claims about canonical totals.','','## Relation meanings','','| Relation | Meaning and limit |','|---|---|','| contains | Navigation hierarchy, not a historical or theological derivation. |','| source | The indexed entry starts on this PDF-page witness. |','| mentions | Literal term match on the listed PDF pages. Front matter, translation notes, and commentary can be included. |','| theme | An editorial tag connecting an indexed unit to a concept. |','| shared narrative / parallel account | A source-attested comparison of accounts; differences are retained. |','| shared wording | Exact English phrase overlap in the supplied translations. |','| traditional affiliation | Veda affiliation stated in the supplied Upanishad compilation; not containment in an independent Samhita PDF. |','| commentary-on | A separately identified commentary on a primary text. |','','## Source audit','','| File | Pages | Text method | Low-text pages |','|---|---:|---|---:|']
for d in a['documents']:lines.append(f"| {d['path'].replace('|','/')} | {d['pages']} | {'Local OCR' if d.get('ocr') else 'Embedded PDF text'} | {len(d['blank_pages'])} |")
lines+=['','A low-text page has fewer than 40 extracted characters. Many are blank leaves, plates, or tables; the original PDF is the authority. All 787 scanned PDF pages were processed locally.','','## Material limits and editorial distinctions','']
seen=set()
for note in a['notes']:
 text=note['text']
 if text in seen:continue
 seen.add(text);lines.append('- '+text)
lines+=['','## Validation and reproducibility','','All node IDs, parent links, edge endpoints, and cited PDF page ranges are validated during the build. Family, work, source edition, and page are separate identities, so duplicated translations and overlapping volumes do not silently become extra canonical works. Original files are identified by SHA-256 in the source manifest.','','See [the validation record](validation.json) for final checks and [browser verification](browser-validation.json) for tested interactions.','','The analysis data remains editable under `analysis/`. `scripts/extract_corpus.py` preserves physical PDF page boundaries; `scripts/run_ocr.py` processes scan-only files locally; the collection analyzers generate index records; `scripts/build_atlas.py` produces JSON, GraphML, and offline browser data. Regenerating the extracted corpus alone overwrites OCR text, so run OCR again or preserve the final corpus when rebuilding.','','Detailed collection notes: [Vedas](vedas-notes.md), [Upanishads](upanishads-notes.md), [Puranas](puranas-notes.md).','','The companion [editable overview board](https://www.tldraw.com/f/dqkvNcwdrQ3GX4MkrPqRB) provides four overview pages. The detailed local atlas contains the complete graph and source-page index.']
(R/'analysis/REPORT.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
